WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.020
Birds are living
dinosaurs, and that shift

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:16.460
is one of the most
spectacular transitions

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.250
in the history of life.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:21.960
This year, researchers studying
both fossils and modern birds

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.060
figured out how one
group of dinosaurs

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.920
became smaller, more adaptable.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.860
The change happened
pretty quickly,

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.860
setting the stage for
the avian explosion--

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.720
the arrival of modern birds.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.850
Think Frankenstein meets
the Fountain of Youth.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.960
Researchers stitched
two mice together--

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.250
one young one
old-- and connected

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:45.370
their circulatory systems.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:47.500
The connection
rejuvenated the brain

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.860
and the muscle of
the older mouse.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:53.400
It appears young blood contains
a factor that can turn back

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.370
the clock on the aging process.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.660
This year, several teams shows
that robots, like people,

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.320
can accomplish more
by working together.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.930
In one study, 1,000 robots, each
the size of American quarter,

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:10.490
joined together like a marching
to form squares and letters

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:11.660
and shapes.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:14.110
Another team,
inspired by termites,

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:16.445
designed a robot
swarm that built

00:01:16.445 --> 00:01:18.650
three-dimensional structures.

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:20.720
Researchers who
make computer chips

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.600
are breaking from
their old ways.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:27.860
In 2014, they created the first
large-scale neuromorphic chips.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:29.910
These chips are designed
to process information

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.510
in ways that are more
like a living brain.

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:34.930
The chips could make it easier
for machines to carry out

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:37.590
perceptual tasks,
such as vision.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:41.360
And more capable robots could
also deal with a lot of data

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:44.390
from sophisticated
environmental sensors.

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:46.230
Scientists who work
with stem cells

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.470
have reached a long-sought goal.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.990
They turned human stem cells
into pancreatic beta cells

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:53.670
that can secrete insulin.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.590
These important
cells are missing

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.186
in people who have
type 1 diabetes.

00:01:58.186 --> 00:02:02.420
But doctors can't yet transplant
the new cells into patients.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:05.790
That's because the cells
would be rejected as foreign.

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:07.950
Still, the achievement
brings a possible treatment

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:11.480
for diabetes one step closer.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:13.480
When it comes to
ancient cave art,

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.550
researchers have long
believed that Europe

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:18.240
held the world's
oldest examples.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.070
But this year,
researchers announced

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:23.590
that a cave in Indonesia holds
hand prints and paintings

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:27.775
that they believe
are 35,000 years old.

00:02:27.775 --> 00:02:30.440
The find suggests that
modern humans were pretty

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:33.480
creative by the time they
left Africa and began

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.560
settling the rest of the world.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.270
In 2013, brain scientists
made a cool but kind

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:42.240
of creepy discovery.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.230
They could instill a fake
memory of fear in mice.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:49.590
To do it, they manipulated
neurons with light.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:53.100
And this year, they took
the method one step further.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.950
They used a similar technique
to switch good memories

00:02:55.950 --> 00:03:01.750
to fearful ones and bad memories
to more pleasant recollections.

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:05.750
In 2014, researchers launched
a record number of cube sats

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:07.370
into space.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:10.890
A cube sat is a pint-sized,
low-cost satellite.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.580
But they're starting to
do some real science.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:15.400
They're cheap and
easy to launch,

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.570
and they can carry
very powerful sensors.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:21.690
That means in time,
constellations of cube sats

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:25.550
may do the job that now takes
a single, complex, and pricey

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:26.930
mission.

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:30.860
The DNA code usually
includes just four letters--

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:35.260
G, C, A, and T. But
this year, researchers

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:39.030
added a new pair of
letters-- X and Y.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:42.080
And they managed to insert
these extra letters into living

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:43.630
bacteria.

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:46.660
Next up, they want to
use the extra DNA letters

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:49.560
to code for amino acids
that are not normally

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:54.550
part of the proteins
that organisms make.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:56.845
And now, the
breakthrough of the year.

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:02.900
That is the sound of a comet.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:05.870
It was recorded by the
Rosetta Spacecraft.

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:08.250
In August, after
a 10-year journey,

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:11.630
Rosetta became the first
probe to orbit a comet.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:14.610
Then, in November, it
made history again,

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:19.610
dropping a small lander that
drifted to the comet's surface.

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:21.680
But the landing was awkward.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:23.680
It bounced and ended
up in a place where

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.340
it couldn't recharge
its solar panels.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:28.240
Still, it collected
a lot of data

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:30.700
before its batteries ran out.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:33.280
Now Rosetta is
chasing the comet.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:36.010
It's going to watch its
jets of gas and dust

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:39.130
turn on as the
comet nears the sun.

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:40.590
The results could
tell scientists

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:43.130
a lot about how our
solar system formed

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:45.910
and what brought water to Earth.

